A year of automation. Two campaigns, both gated, both measured
against a holdout, both read back through reasoning.as_evidence --
so R10 is satisfied and every observation here is controlled.

  send a manager   costs 2000, and truly recovers 0.35
  cut the price    costs 2000, and truly recovers 0.05

Against a loss of 16835 an action of cost 2000 pays for itself at a
recovery of 0.119. So the first is worth doing and the second is not,
and the whole job of a calibration is to say so.

=== PART A: each campaign calibrated from its own evidence

  send a manager    estimate 0.368   interval 0.297 to 0.438
                    recommendation send a manager  ACT   assurance 1

  cut the price     estimate 0.027   interval -0.057 to 0.112
                    recommendation do nothing      DO NOT ACT   assurance 1

  Both right. This is what the library does when it is asked one
  question at a time.

=== PART B: one pool -- "how well do our interventions work?"

  Nothing in the shape of the data objects. Twenty-four controlled
  outcomes, all revenue, all from gated actions, all measured the
  same way. A loop that stores every measured action and calibrates
  from the store produces exactly this.

  send a manager    estimate 0.197   interval 0.108 to 0.287
                    recommendation send a manager  ACT   assurance 0.9

  cut the price     estimate 0.197   interval 0.108 to 0.287
                    recommendation cut the price   ACT   assurance 0.9

  THE PRICE CUT IS NOW RECOMMENDED, and it recovers 0.05 against a
  break-even of 0.119. The estimate is not a compromise between two
  answers -- it is the answer to a question nobody asked, and it is
  wrong in opposite directions for the two campaigns that made it.

=== PART C: and more evidence does not help

     n     estimate    95% interval          width
  12    0.18       0.063 to 0.297     0.234
  24    0.197       0.108 to 0.287     0.178
  48    0.19       0.128 to 0.252     0.125
  120   0.198       0.161 to 0.235     0.074

  The interval collapses towards the midpoint of two truths, which is
  the effect of nothing. THE STANDARD ERROR FALLS WITH THE SIZE OF THE
  POOL, so the more incommensurable evidence is gathered the more
  confident the wrong number becomes -- and by n=120 the interval no
  longer contains either truth at all:

  cut the price     estimate 0.198   interval 0.161 to 0.235
                    recommendation cut the price   ACT   assurance 1

  DECISIVE, from 120 controlled observations, about an intervention
  that loses money every time it is taken. That is the shape recipe 9
  taught us to trust -- a narrow interval well clear of the break-even
  -- and here it is being produced by a defect.

=== PART D: what the evidence can be made to say

  pooling two interventions:
    decision.calibrate: item 13 is about cut the price and item 1 about send a manager -- calibrating one intervention from another's outcomes answers a question nobody asked, confidently (design R16)

  pooling two measures:
    decision.calibrate: item 13 measures days_to_pay and item 1 measures revenue -- these are not observations of one quantity, and their average is not an estimate of anything (design R16)

  AND THE CONTROL, because a rule that refuses everything is not a
  rule. The same intervention on the same measure in DIFFERENT PLACES
  is exactly what a calibration is for, and it still pools:
    12 outcomes from two regions, estimate 0.353, about send a manager on revenue

  WHAT MADE THE REFUSAL POSSIBLE WAS NOT A NEW MEASUREMENT. It was
  the chain: a Decision now names the Finding it came from, an Action
  carries the Decision and the policy that permitted it, and evidence
  read back off an Action can therefore say what it was about. §9
  had asked for that from the start and called it "not a logging
  feature" -- this is what it was for.
